/**
 * C1: LEADING (docs/43-lessons.md §15.3, part 5; docs/reviews/c1-cast.md).
 *
 * Spring of the family's third year. Kristi moves on to a bigger team at
 * another firm, and the management asks Tom to lead his. The part is his first
 * weeks of it: the news and what changes, interviewing Ott and the minute about
 * yourself that every interview and the C1 speaking exam open on, the hard
 * feedback Marko has coming, a client who wants the app two weeks sooner, the
 * proposal Tom writes for the board, and the presentation where the director
 * cuts in. The pattern is emphasis: the news goes last, and `just`, `nimelt`,
 * `isegi` and `alles` point at the word that matters, which is how Estonian
 * stresses what English stresses with the voice; around it, one thing said
 * three ways for three rooms, and the question nobody is meant to answer. The
 * conversation move is interrupting politely, and taking the floor back when
 * you are interrupted.
 *
 * Every conversation heard first is joined halfway or sped up (`rampC1.ts`,
 * band 3); the talk taken down as notes is a guest on the morning show, heard
 * once; every text read is asked a gap. Names used in one lesson only: Kaisa,
 * the client (from B2's `b2-26-probably`), Heiki, the morning show's guest,
 * and the director and the finance director of the board, by role.
 *
 * A preview part (`preview.ts`) until C1 is the course. The texts read and the
 * talk are read whole by the native speaker (§15.13 C1-7); the rest by flagged
 * line. Held to every check a lesson is (`npx tsx scripts/check-lesson-part.ts
 * --part c1-juht`).
 */

